From 9fc697611e5c70148c4602d29eb5f1c912f1b2b7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 06:55:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoRenderers/gles: each eye of a stereoscopic source gets
 its part of the frame

CBaseRenderer::ManageRenderArea() takes the left or the right half (or
the top or the bottom one) of a stereoscopic frame for the eye that is
being drawn, by the stereo mode in m_iFlags. The OpenGL renderer sets
those flags when it is configured. The OpenGL ES one never did, so no
eye was ever given its half: the monoscopic mode showed the whole
double picture, and the split modes of the base render system and the
anaglyph modes that Hades added to the OpenGL ES one drew it once for
each eye.

The flags are now set as the OpenGL renderer sets them. And the source
rectangles of the planes, which this renderer worked out once, when a
picture was uploaded, are worked out again for a picture that is
already loaded: the second eye is drawn from the same buffer with
another rectangle. The renderer for VAAPI pictures already did so on
every call, and so does the OpenGL renderer.

A source that is not stereoscopic has no stereo mode: its flags have no
stereo bit, and nothing reads the others. One thing changes for it, for
the better: a picture that is drawn again (paused) while its source
rectangle changes (a zoomed picture clipped by a preview window) now
follows that rectangle at once, not with the next picture.

Limit: video that is drawn as a layer of its own (HDR output) is drawn
once, outside the per-eye passes: no crop and no anaglyph there, as
before. A stereoscopic source is never given native Dolby Vision
output.

Found by reading the Blu-ray 3D work on the main branch of the Intel
Dolby Vision LibreELEC fork (CroqueMr/libreelec-x86-DV, d9ad989ee928,
published as R1.0.0 Beta3), which carries the same correction with the
3D patches of cinema-ONE and popcornmix.

Read in the code and reviewed; nothing draws this in a host test, and
it has not been seen on a display.
---
 .../VideoPlayer/VideoRenderers/LinuxRendererGLES.cpp      | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/xbmc/cores/VideoPlayer/VideoRenderers/LinuxRendererGLES.cpp b/xbmc/cores/VideoPlayer/VideoRenderers/LinuxRendererGLES.cpp
index a05238e..5643391 100644
--- a/xbmc/cores/VideoPlayer/VideoRenderers/LinuxRendererGLES.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/cores/VideoPlayer/VideoRenderers/LinuxRendererGLES.cpp
@@ -193,6 +193,11 @@ bool CLinuxRendererGLES::Configure(const VideoPicture &picture, float fps, unsig
   m_sourceHeight = picture.iHeight;
   m_renderOrientation = orientation;
 
+  // The stereo mode is what makes ManageRenderArea() take one half of a stereoscopic frame for
+  // each eye. The OpenGL renderer sets the same flags.
+  m_iFlags =
+      GetFlagsChromaPosition(picture.chroma_position) | GetFlagsStereoMode(picture.stereoMode);
+
   m_srcPrimaries = picture.color_primaries;
   m_srcColorBits = picture.colorBits;
   m_srcFullRange = picture.color_range == 1;
@@ -1300,6 +1305,9 @@ bool CLinuxRendererGLES::UploadTexture(int index)
 
   if (m_buffers[index].loaded)
   {
+    // ManageRenderArea() moves the source rectangle between the two eyes of a stereoscopic
+    // source, which are drawn from the same buffer: the rectangles of its planes follow
+    CalculateTextureSourceRects(index, 3);
     return true;
   }
 
